(To configure and program the NodeMCU ESP32 using the Arduino IDE, build a traffic light system, and) Tj
T* (establish UART serial communication to transmit the system's state to a Raspberry Pi Pico.) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 12 Tf
13.7999999999999989 TL
0. g
56.6929133858267775 632.1262204724408775 Td
(Introduction:) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10 Tf
11.5 TL
0. g
56.6929133858267775 615.1183464566928478 Td
(In complex embedded systems, multiple microcontrollers often need to communicate. This project builds a) Tj
T* (traffic light controller on the ESP32 and uses the UART \(Universal Asynchronous Receiver-Transmitter\)) Tj
T* (protocol to stream state changes to a Raspberry Pi Pico over a two-wire connection \(TX/RX\).) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 12 Tf
13.7999999999999989 TL
0. g
56.6929133858267775 549.9214960629921052 Td
(1. Theoretical Concepts) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10 Tf
11.5 TL
0. g
56.6929133858267775 532.9136220472440755 Td
(UART Serial Communication: UART is a hardware communication protocol that uses asynchronous serial) Tj
T* (communication with configurable speeds \(baud rates\). In our setup, the ESP32 acts as the transmitter \(TX\)) Tj
T* (and the Pico as the receiver \(RX\) running at 9600 baud.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Ground Loop: A critical aspect of serial communication between two discrete boards is ensuring they share a) Tj
T* (common Ground \(GND\). Without a common ground reference, the voltages representing 1s and 0s become) Tj
T* (erratic, resulting in corrupted data.) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 11 Tf
12.6499999999999986 TL
0. g
56.6929133858267775 411.0238582677164914 Td
(2. Source Code - ESP32 \(Transmitter\)) Tj
ET
